2026-09-28: small completion-integrity control, NOT ordering-failure closure.

ublk-aio-completion-order.c removes fio and bpftrace. On the unchanged null
target, it sends ascending 4-KiB direct writes in bounded batches at depths
1/16, with 1/2 hardware queues. Every returned AIO request identity, object
pointer, result length and uniqueness is checked. Reordering is observed, not
required to be absent except in the depth-one control. Null storage does not
establish persistence or stored-data correctness.

GCC-built diagnostic on GCC and EvoCC kernels: 4/4 checks per Image PASS,
65,536 successful writes per Image, no missing/duplicate/short completions.
All eight combinations observe zero displaced/backward completions. Therefore
this small batched workload has NOT reproduced the original fio/trace failure;
do not claim a kernel or compiler repair, or original generic_01/02 PASS.
Next isolate continuously refilled submission and the trace observer itself.

GCC14 and Clang19 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror compilation passes in the sealed
ARM64 image. Only the GCC diagnostic binary is executed; Clang object compilation
is not a Clang runtime claim. The diagnostic init reuses selftest-init.c's
root switch, nonce, timeout and shutdown, and invokes a preopened separate
executable. It does not rewrite an upstream program or the snapshot backing.
Both boot/shutdown predicates PASS; both 4/5-GiB backing hashes stay unchanged.
The payload is under 2 MiB; there is no new runtime disk or workspace clone.

Frozen Documentation/block/blk-mq.rst:124-126 states that block/device
protocols do not guarantee completion order. That general boundary alone does
not establish the exact cause of this null-target/selftest discrepancy.

Inputs/build/replay: .build/floating-targeted/ublk-order-20260928/
  build.py uses gcc-14 static probe + shared init and gen_init_cpio -t 0;
  replay.py uses selftest.flight, hvf-host, KUnit autorun off, QEMU drift explicit.
  build-initial.* preserves the wrapper's initial renamed-main return warning;
  the wrapper now declares the existing nonreturning poweroff routine correctly.

